  {
    "id": "2050710930455941599",
    "created_at": "2026-05-02 18:56:00 -04:00",
    "full_text": "That’s a good question, It doesn’t. That’s the whole point. The curve self-regulates. Doubling staked ETH doesn’t double security, the marginal gain flattens. The dampening factor just encodes that into the math so the protocol stops overpaying for security it already has.\n\nThe dampening factor (1+D/k) encodes this diminishing return directly into the reward function.\n\nThe parameter k (set at 2^26, ~67M ETH) defines where the curve peaks. Below that, issuance still grows. Above that, it starts declining. \n\nBased on the proposal the idea is to start with k=2^26 (~67M ETH), evaluate the effects on stake composition and quantity, then potentially adjust down to k=2^25 (~33.6M ETH) as the final setting.",
    "media": [],
